## Work kinds

**This table is what makes the skill work against your provider.** The shipped rubric
decides one of seven neutral *work kinds* for each block — it can't know what your
account calls them, because no two accounts spell their tasks the same way. Fill in the
right-hand column with your provider's exact task names and the mapping is done.

Leave a row blank and that work kind falls back to the default task above. Leave the whole
table out and the skill matches each work kind against the tasks your projects actually
offer, then proposes the row it worked out — so an existing `.context.md` keeps working and
fills this in as you go.

| Work kind | Your task |
|---|---|
| `Meeting` | `<task name>` |
| `Development` | `<task name>` |
| `Documentation` | `<task name>` |
| `Project management` | `<task name>` |
| `Testing` | `<task name>` |
| `Investigation` | `<task name>` |
| `Internal admin` | `<a non-billable task>` |

A name here only decides *which* task to reach for. Whether it exists on a given project,
and whether it's billable, comes from that project's own task assignments — the skill
checks, and substitutes with a note when your task isn't offered there.

